\section{The predictor layer}\label{sec:pred}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:centered}
$\sum_{g\in G}(r(g)-1)=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
This is Theorem~\ref{thm:mean}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero covariance]\label{thm:cov}
$\sum_{(g,h)\in G\times H}(r_G(g)-1)(r_H(h)-1)=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The sum factors as a product of two sums, each zero by Lemma~\ref{lem:centered}.
\end{proof}

Via Theorem~\ref{thm:crt}: for coprime moduli $m,n$, the local rates $r_m(N)$ and $r_n(N)$ are exactly uncorrelated over the units modulo $mn$.

\begin{theorem}[Two-modulus score variance]\label{thm:score}
For integers $a,b$,
\[\sum_{(g,h)}\bigl(a(r_G(g)-1)+b(r_H(h)-1)\bigr)^2=|G|\,|H|\bigl(a^2(t(G)-1)+b^2(t(H)-1)\bigr).\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand the square. The cross term vanishes by Theorem~\ref{thm:cov}. Each square term reduces to Corollary~\ref{cor:var} times the order of the other group.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two-prime Euler score]\label{thm:euler}
Let $p\neq q$ be odd primes, $a,b\in\Z$, and $S(N)=a\,r_p(N)+b\,r_q(N)$ for units $N$ modulo $pq$. Then
\[\sum_N S(N)=\varphi(pq)(a+b),\qquad \sum_N\bigl(S(N)-(a+b)\bigr)^2=\varphi(pq)(a^2+b^2).\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Transport the sums to $(\Z/p)^\times\times(\Z/q)^\times$ via the CRT. Use Theorem~\ref{thm:mean} in each factor for the mean, and Theorem~\ref{thm:score} with $t=2$ for the variance.
\end{proof}

By induction, a score $\sum_p w_p\,r_p(N)$ over distinct odd primes has mean $\sum w_p$ and variance $\sum w_p^2$. The regression of yield on Euler bits is diagonal.

\section{Experimental evidence}\label{sec:exp}

\subsection{Design}
Four cells in $u$, each with $100{,}000$ values, were generated with a fixed seed. Three populations were compared:
(1)~sieve values $x^2-N$ pooled over many random $N$;
(2)~unrestricted random integers of the same sizes;
(3)~random integers counted as smooth only over the primes $p\le B$ with $\leg{N}{p}=+1$ (the ``half factor base'' model).
The pre-registered hypothesis H1 was that (1) behaves like (3).

\subsection{Ensemble results}
Populations (1) and (2) agreed within noise in every cell, both at $0.87$--$0.99$ of $\rho(u)$, a known finite-size factor. Population (3) was smooth $21$--$56$ times less often. H1 is refuted. Theorem~\ref{thm:main} explains why: population (3) keeps the halving and drops the doubling.

\subsection{Per-$N$ results}
The correlation between the per-$N$ smooth rate and $\#\{p\le100\text{ odd}:\leg{N}{p}=+1\}$ was $0.50,0.45,0.48,0.40$ across the cells. The top-to-bottom decile spread was $2.4\times$ at $u=2.5$ and $9.3\times$ at $u=3.5$. Qualitatively this matches Theorems~\ref{thm:main} and~\ref{thm:euler}: the variance is driven by small-prime Legendre bits, accumulates additively, and grows with the number of relevant primes.

\subsection{Resolution of the earlier anomaly}
The earlier ratios $0.54$--$0.76$ came from one $N$ per scale. Given a decile spread of $2.4$--$9.3\times$, they reflect draw-to-draw variation rather than a systematic deficit. The effective-$u$ explanation is retired.

\subsection{Small-scale replication}
A small replication used $40$ fourteen-digit $N$, $1{,}500$ values each, $B=600$ and mean $u\approx3.8$. It gives a mean-rate ratio $\approx0.9$ against size-matched random integers, a $\approx26\times$ deficit for the half-base model, a $\approx10\times$ decile spread, and a correlation $\approx0.58$ with the residue count. These figures are illustrative only.

\section{Algorithms and applications}\label{sec:alg}

\subsection{Exact local-moment table}
For distinct odd primes $p_1,\dots,p_k$ with $M=\prod p_i$, compute $r_M(N)=\prod_i(1+\leg{N}{p_i})$ for each unit $N$. Each $N$ costs $O(k\log M)$ multiplications. Compare the totals with $\varphi(M)$ and $\varphi(M)(2^k-1)$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:supp} the outcome is always the two-point law $\{0:1-2^{-k},\ 2^k:2^{-k}\}$.

\subsection{A priori yield predictor}
Given $N$, small odd primes $p_1<\dots<p_P$ (e.g.\ $P=20$) and weights $w_p$:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item compute $\epsilon_i=\leg{N}{p_i}$ via $N^{(p_i-1)/2}\bmod p_i$;
\item return $S(N)=\sum_i w_{p_i}(1+\epsilon_i)$.
\end{enumerate}
The cost is negligible next to sieving. Over random $N$ the score has mean $\sum w_p$ and variance $\sum w_p^2$. The unweighted count up to $100$ already correlates at $0.40$--$0.50$ with yield. A natural weighting is $w_p=\log p/(p-1)$.

\subsection{Multiplier selection as variance harvesting}
The multiplier technique sieves $kN$ for a small squarefree $k$ that makes many small primes residues. By Theorem~\ref{thm:main} no choice raises the mean local rate. A multiplier helps by selecting a favourable draw from a high-variance distribution.

\section{Discussion}
Sections~\ref{sec:group}--\ref{sec:pred} are exact statements about local divisibility counts over complete residue systems of $N$. Section~\ref{sec:exp} is empirical. The bridge from local rates to smoothness at a given $u$ would require a Dickman-type transfer principle, incorporating the finite-size factor; we do not claim one.

The mean identity holds in every finite abelian group because fibres partition the domain, which is why no penalty appears at any depth. The variance $t-1=2^k-1$ is governed by the two-torsion subgroup. Each new prime doubles the jackpot and halves its probability. Our formulas treat $N$ as uniform modulo $M$, and the prime $2$ and prime powers are excluded from the clean statements.

\section{Future work and conjectures}

\begin{conjecture}[Log-normal yield law]
For fixed $u$, $\log$(per-$N$ smooth yield) is asymptotically Gaussian over random $N$. Its mean is the unrestricted Dickman value, with its finite-size factor, and its variance is $\sim c(u)\sum_{p\le B}(\log p/(p-1))^2$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Variance--$u$ monotonicity]
The per-$N$ decile spread is strictly increasing in $u$ and exceeds $30\times$ at $u=4.5$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Optimal Euler predictor]
Among scores $\sum_{p\le P}w_p\leg{N}{p}$, the best linear predictor of yield has $w_p\propto\log p/(p-1)$, and $P=20$ recovers at least $85\%$ of the achievable correlation.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{direction}[Multiplier selection]
Quantify the expected gain of the best of the first $K$ squarefree multipliers as an extreme-value statistic of the yield distribution.
\end{direction}

\begin{direction}[Transfer principle]
Prove a Dickman-type transfer from local moments to smoothness probabilities.
\end{direction}

\section{Conclusion}
The quadratic-residue condition imposes no penalty on average. At every sieving modulus the mean local divisibility rate of $x^2-N$ equals that of a random integer, because the fibres of the squaring map partition a finite abelian group. What the condition produces is a variance growing exponentially in the number of sieving primes, built from exactly uncorrelated Legendre bits. It is visible as a $2.4$--$9.3\times$ per-$N$ spread in yield, accounts for an earlier anomaly, and is cheaply predictable by Euler's criterion. The bite is variance, not mean.

